Yes, there is no age ceiling for a child who is considered permanently and totally disabled. According to IRS Publication 17 (2025), a child can qualify as a dependent regardless of their age if they are permanently and totally disabled at any time during the year. The criteria for a qualifying child are as follows:

1. Under age 19 at the end of the year and younger than you (or your spouse if filing jointly);
2. A student under age 24 at the end of the year and younger than you (or your spouse);
3. Any age if permanently and totally disabled.

This means that if a child meets the criteria of being permanently and totally disabled, they can be a qualifying child regardless of how old they are. 

For more details, you can refer to IRS Pub 17 (2025), Chapter 3 on Dependents.

